By this point, the environment had changed significantly from my first DQN attempt.

I had:

- constrained the action space so exploration focused on meaningful questions;
- redesigned the observation around dynamic split ratios;
- introduced rewards based on logarithmic information reduction;
- added a small cost for taking unnecessary steps;
- and established heuristic baselines showing what efficient behaviour could look like.

The next step was straightforward in theory:

> Train the agent and see whether it could learn.

I already had a game constraint in place:

max_steps = 20

That limit made sense.

The strongest heuristic agents could solve the larger search spaces in roughly 12 steps, while 20 steps provided some room for less efficient strategies.

So I initially used the same limit during RL training.

But this introduced another problem.

The 20-Step Limit Made Sense for Evaluation

The purpose of the original limit was to measure efficiency.

I did not want an agent to:

Ask question
    ↓
Ask another weak question
    ↓
Ask another
    ↓
Continue indefinitely

At some point, the episode needs to end.

A trained agent should be judged partly by whether it can solve the problem within a reasonable number of decisions.

For benchmarking, max_steps = 20 gave me a clear constraint:

Can the agent identify the character?

AND

Can it do so efficiently?

But I started realizing that these were evaluation questions.

They were not necessarily the right conditions for learning.

An Untrained Agent Does Not Know How to Be Efficient Yet

At the beginning of training, the DQN has no useful policy.

Even after fixing the action space and improving the observation, the agent still needs time to discover useful trajectories.

Early behaviour may look like:

Question A
    ↓
Poor reduction

Question B
    ↓
Small reduction

Question C
    ↓
Useful reduction

Question D
    ↓
Another poor decision

A trained agent might eventually learn to reach the target efficiently.

But an untrained agent has to experience both:

bad trajectories;
and successful trajectories.

With a strict 20-step cutoff, I noticed a problem.

The agent could spend much of its early training exploring inefficient sequences and then get truncated before ever reaching a successful terminal state.

The agent receives the experience:

That sequence did not finish in time.

But if successful episodes are extremely rare, it has much less opportunity to learn:

This is what eventually reaching the goal looks like.

That distinction became important.

Training Needs Successful Experience

The agent cannot begin by behaving like the final policy I want to evaluate.

It needs to learn how to get there.

This made me rethink the role of the step limit.

Instead of treating:

max_steps = 20

as an absolute property of the environment, I separated two purposes.

Training

Give the agent enough time to explore and complete trajectories.

Evaluation

Measure whether the learned policy can solve the task efficiently under stricter conditions.

These are related environments, but they do not necessarily need identical episode limits.

Extending the Training Horizon

To give the agent more opportunity to reach successful terminal states, I increased the training horizon:

Training:
max_steps = 100

The purpose was not to teach the agent that taking 100 steps was acceptable.

The small step penalty was still present.

The agent was still encouraged to finish efficiently.

But it was no longer immediately cut off after 20 decisions while it was still learning.

The training process now looked more like:

Episode starts
      ↓
Agent explores
      ↓
Makes inefficient decisions
      ↓
Continues interacting
      ↓
Eventually narrows the search space
      ↓
Experiences successful completion
      ↓
Learns from the full trajectory

This gave the learning process room to develop.

Efficiency Was Still Part of the Objective

Increasing the training horizon created an obvious concern:

If the agent has 100 steps, won't it just learn to take 100 steps?

That is where the reward design mattered.

The agent still received a cost for each additional step.

Conceptually:

Every action:
    small negative reward

Useful information:
    positive reward

Successful completion:
    terminal reward

So the training environment was more forgiving in terms of how long an episode was allowed to continue.

But the reward still made unnecessary behaviour costly.

The distinction was:

Training horizon:
How long the agent is allowed to keep trying.

Reward:
How strongly the agent is encouraged to finish efficiently.

Those are not the same thing.

This was an important conceptual separation for me.

Training and Evaluation Became Two Different Experiments

I eventually started thinking about the process like this:

The training environment asks:

Can the agent learn a useful policy?

The evaluation environment asks:

How good is the policy it learned?

Using the same constraint for both had initially seemed natural.

But they serve different purposes.

The codebase now involved:

Environment
     ↓
Observation space
     ↓
RL model
     ↓
Agent wrapper
     ↓
Simulation
     ↓
Evaluation
     ↓
Visualization

A change in one layer could expose assumptions in another.

The modular structure I had built earlier became increasingly useful as the project became more complex.

Forcing a Final Guess

There was still another practical problem.

Even with the longer training horizon, I did not want an evaluation episode to continue indefinitely when the agent had already narrowed the search space significantly.

At some point, continuing to ask questions stops making sense.

I introduced fallback logic to handle situations such as:

Remaining candidates ≤ 5

or:

Approaching the evaluation step limit

In those situations, the system could force a transition toward a direct guess.

This was partly a practical design decision.

The goal was to prevent the agent from wasting the final stages of an episode when the search space was already very small.

This also made the evaluation process more predictable.

What This Changed in My Thinking

Before this experiment, I had mostly thought about the environment as one fixed object.

The game has rules.

The agent trains inside those rules.

The agent is evaluated inside those same rules.

But training made that view feel too simplistic.

I started seeing an RL experiment as having multiple layers.

The task

What is the agent fundamentally trying to solve?

In this case:

Identify the hidden character through sequential yes/no decisions.

The training environment

What conditions give the agent a realistic opportunity to learn that task?

In this case:

a longer episode horizon;
meaningful observations;
constrained exploration;
information-based rewards.
The evaluation environment

What conditions measure the quality of the learned policy?

In this case:

stricter step limits;
win rate;
average steps;
search-space contraction;
comparison against heuristic baselines.

The underlying task remained the same.

But the conditions used for learning and measurement did not have to be identical.

The Bigger Lesson

This stage taught me something that seems obvious in hindsight but was not obvious when I first started building the environment:

A difficult evaluation condition can be useful precisely because it is difficult. That does not mean it is always useful during early learning.

If an agent is evaluated under a strict constraint, failure is informative.

But if the same constraint prevents the agent from ever experiencing meaningful success during training, it can interfere with learning itself.

The goal was not to make training easy.

It was to make successful learning trajectories possible while still rewarding efficiency.

By this point, the RL experiment had gone through several iterations.

The first action space

Failed because direct guesses dominated exploration.

Action constraints

Redirected exploration toward the actual deduction problem.

Blind observations

Failed because the agent could not adapt to the changing search space.

Raw observations

Risked exposing too much of the underlying structure.

Dynamic split ratios

Provided an abstract representation of the current decision landscape.

Linear elimination rewards

Failed to distinguish small relative progress from major uncertainty reduction.

Information-based rewards

Measured progress through logarithmic search-space reduction.

Strict training horizon

Risked truncating learning trajectories before the agent could experience enough successful outcomes.

Extended training horizon

Gave the agent room to complete trajectories while step penalties continued to reward efficiency.

The project had become much more iterative than I originally expected.

The Next Stage

At this point, I had a trainable environment and a more complete experimentation pipeline.

But an experiment is only useful if I can inspect what happened.

Training an agent and printing:

Finished training.

does not tell me very much.

I needed to compare agents, run repeated simulations, track metrics, and visualize their behaviour.

The next stage of the project focused on turning the system into something I could actually experiment with.

That meant building the evaluation and visualization layer:

repeated simulations;
configurable epochs;
override probabilities;
win rates;
number of steps;
search-space contraction;
and plots that made the differences between strategies visible.
